
// **********            FunctionTransformer            ************

Definition:
FunctionTransformer is a Feature Transformation technique used to apply a mathematical function to existing features.

******** IMPORTANT : Function Transformer or any transformer are used to make the coloumn such that its distribution is like bell shaped (normal form) because
                     our machine learning models perform well on bell shaped distribution , where as standardization helps to bring the data on a scale such that
                     it is easy to develop equal footing with corresponding columns so only one coloumn doesnt dominate rigorously
***********
Why:
Sometimes data contains highly skewed values. We transform these values to make their distribution more suitable for ML models.

Common Transformations:
-> np.log1p(x)  : Log Transformation
-> np.sqrt(x)   : Square Root Transformation
-> np.square(x) : Squaring
-> 1 / x        : Reciprocal (x must not be zero)

Formula:
-> Log Transformation: x' = log(1 + x)

Important points:
-> Log transformation is useful for reducing RIGHT SKEWNESS.
-> It compresses large values more than small values.
-> It does NOT guarantee better model accuracy.
-> StandardScaler changes the SCALE, but does NOT remove skewness.
-> FunctionTransformer changes the SHAPE when the chosen function does so.
-> Compare results on validation data.

// **********  StandardScaler vs FunctionTransformer  ************

// My Understanding:

// 1. FunctionTransformer
-> Used when we want to change the distribution of a feature.
-> Example: Salary contains extremely large values, so we apply
   log transformation to reduce right skewness.
-> It can be applied to one or multiple columns.

// 2. StandardScaler
-> Used to bring numerical features onto a comparable scale.
-> Example: Age ranges from 18-60, but Salary ranges from
   20,000-10,00,000.
-> Scaling can help algorithms like KNN and SVM.

// 3. Can we use both?
-> YES! We can apply log transformation to Salary first,
   then StandardScaler to Age and transformed Salary.
-> This addresses two different problems:
   FunctionTransformer -> Distribution / shape
   StandardScaler      -> Scale

// Memory Trick:
-> Skewness problem = Consider a transformation.
-> Scale problem = Consider a scaler.
-> Both problems = We may use both!

