X_transformed = transformer.fit_transform(X)


// **********            Power Transformer          ***********

Definition: Power Transformer (Box-Cox and Yeo-Johnson) automatically finds the best power (λ) to make a skewed column look more like a normal distribution.

Why: In Function Transformer we guess the function by trial and error. Power Transformer learns the best λ from the data for us.

Formula (Box-Cox):  y = (x^λ - 1) / λ,   when λ = 0 then y = log(x)

-> λ = 1 no change, 0.5 square root, 0 log, -1 reciprocal, 2 square. So earlier functions are special cases of this.
-> Best λ = the one that makes data most bell-shaped (found from the data).

Box-Cox vs Yeo-Johnson:
-> Box-Cox: only x > 0
-> Yeo-Johnson: any x (positive, zero, negative), safer choice
-> Not sure? use Yeo-Johnson.

Yeo-Johnson formula (4 cases):
-> x >= 0, λ != 0 :  ((x + 1)^λ - 1) / λ
-> x >= 0, λ = 0  :  log(x + 1)
-> x < 0,  λ != 2 :  -((-x + 1)^(2 - λ) - 1) / (2 - λ)
-> x < 0,  λ = 2  :  -log(-x + 1)
-> Positive side = Box-Cox on (x + 1), negative side = mirrored version.

Important points:
-> It learns λ from data, so fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.
-> By default sklearn also standardizes the output (mean 0, std 1).
-> Check before and after with histogram or QQ plot.
-> Not needed for tree models.

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import PowerTransformer

pt = PowerTransformer(method="yeo-johnson")   # or "box-cox"
X_train_t = pt.fit_transform(X_train)         # learns λ from train only
X_test_t = pt.transform(X_test)               # reuses train's λ

pt.lambdas_                                   # λ of each column

# QQ plot check
import scipy.stats as stats
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
stats.probplot(X_train["Fare"], dist="norm", plot=plt)



// **********            Binning and Binarization          ***********

Problem: Numerical columns have many values, outliers and uneven spread. Often we only care about the range or a yes/no, not the exact value.

// Binning (Discretization)
Definition: Converts a continuous column into groups (bins). Ex: Age -> Child, Teenager, Adult, Senior.

Why: Simplifies data, captures range patterns, handles outliers (they fall in the first or last bin).

Types (strategy):
-> Equal Width (uniform): same width for every bin
-> Equal Frequency (quantile): same number of values in every bin
-> KMeans: bins from clusters of similar values

Important points:
-> Fit only on train data, transform on both train and test, otherwise it is DATA LEAKAGE.
-> Input must be 2D, so use [["Age"]].

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import KBinsDiscretizer

kbin = KBinsDiscretizer(n_bins=5, encode="ordinal", strategy="quantile")
X_train_t = kbin.fit_transform(X_train[["Age"]])
X_test_t = kbin.transform(X_test[["Age"]])
kbin.bin_edges_

-> encode = "ordinal", "onehot", "onehot-dense"
-> strategy = "uniform", "quantile", "kmeans"


// Binarization
Definition: Converts values into 0 or 1 using a threshold (above threshold = 1, rest = 0). Ex: Age > 18 -> 1.

Why: Creates a yes/no indicator.

Code:
from sklearn.preprocessing import Binarizer

bz = Binarizer(threshold=10)
X_train_b = bz.fit_transform(X_train[["Fare"]])

Remember:
-> Binning = Values -> Groups
-> Binarization = Values -> 0 or 1

// **********            Handling Mixed Variables          ***********

Problem: Some columns hold both numbers and text, so the model can't use them as they are. Ex: Titanic Cabin -> A15, B22, C85.

Definition: A mixed variable is a column that contains both numerical and categorical values.

Types:
-> Type 1: numbers and text in separate rows (a value is either number or text). Ex: 1, 2, A, B, 3
-> Type 2: text and number in the same value. Ex: Cabin "A15" (letter A + number 15)

Solution: Split the column into two new columns, one numerical and one categorical.

Why:
-> Each part gets the right treatment (scaling for numerical, encoding for categorical)
-> Each part may carry useful information (cabin letter = deck, number = position)

Important points:
-> Split first, then apply normal feature engineering on the new columns.
-> NaN in the original stays NaN in the new columns.
-> Some cabins have many values ("C23 C25 C27"), we take only the first.

Code:

df["cabin_letter"] = df["Cabin"].str[0]                             # first letter
df["cabin_num"] = df["Cabin"].str.extract(r"(\d+)").astype(float)   # the digits

Remember: Mixed variable = split into number part + text part
